(function(){
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------
  // EARLY CHAPTER SCENES. Arcs II to V (the early game) were written before chapters got their reading modal, so marking one
  // read only gave a toast. Each now shows the chapter's scene in the same "Continue" popup every later arc uses, built from the
  // chapter's own comic page. Arc VI already has its own scenes, and Arc I has the read-back. The scene shows once, the moment the
  // chapter is first marked read (or, for a fight chapter, the moment the fight is won). For ordinary chapters the scene comes first and the chapter is marked read
  // (XP, reputation, recruits) when Continue is pressed; the Story screen's button says "Read Chapter".
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------
  const SCENES = {
    2: {
      1: "For once, the sea asks nothing of them.<br><br>Supplies are counted, rations stowed, the crew settled into the rhythm of the ship. Joel says the weather is in their favour, and San says it is a good day to sail. Soel sits at the rail with his ears turned toward the water, as though listening for something only he can hear.<br><br>The Crimson Tide leaves the port behind, carrying more than cargo. It carries a family.",
      2: "Days of steady wind and clear skies, and the whole crew has started to relax. That is exactly what worries Joel. Calm seas can be deceptive, he says, and it is when you relax that you miss what matters.<br><br>Soel has been restless since morning. He will not stop staring at the water, as if something had moved beneath it.<br><br>San looks down and sees only waves, and sunlight dancing on the surface. Then, for a moment, something shifts.<br><br>“Do you see that?”",
      3: "The new trade routes are quiet. The sea is calm. Or so they thought.<br><br>The lookout lowers her binoculars and does not say anything for a long moment. There is something out there, faint and far, a smudge on the horizon that does not belong. It is moving in the same direction as the Crimson Tide.<br><br>It might be a mirage. It might be trouble. Either way, Joel doubles the watch, and Soel, who has been quiet all day, stays very still.",
      4: "On the fourth day, the horizon gives them a surprise: a ship where no ship should be. There is no flag, no light, no sign of anyone aboard. The nearest port is days away, and the winds have not been in its favour.<br><br>Its sails hang in tatters and its hull is a shadow. Yet it follows the same line as the Crimson Tide, not passing and not crossing. Following.<br><br>“Keep your distance,” San says. “We do not know what it is.”",
      5: "The ship is empty. No crew, no movement. The deck is silent and the air is heavy. Even the wind seems to hold its breath.<br><br>They split into pairs and search it. Meals still sit on the table and tools hang on their racks. Everything is in place, as if the crew had intended to return.<br><br>San finds a mark she does not recognise, from no trade guild she knows. Down the dark corridor, Soel lets out a low, uneasy growl.<br><br>This was not an empty ship. It was a message.",
      6: "The ship is in good condition, but nobody is here. It feels as if they left in a hurry. Or as if they never really left.<br><br>In the captain’s cabin, a ledger lies intact among the dust and salt. Names and roles, a crew once alive, now only ink on paper. Some of the names feel familiar, though no one can say why.<br><br>The last entry is brief. <i>We wait for the wind. Same sea, different tomorrow.</i><br><br>Names fade from the world. But not from the sea.",
      7: "It starts as a feeling, a weight in the air. Soel pauses with his ears twitching.<br><br>Then come the voices, soft and in pieces, no louder than breath. <i>Water. Home. Forgive.</i> Eliz kneels beside him and asks if he can hear it. Soel watches the empty corridor, and for a moment his whiskers tremble. <i>Stay. My crew. Not yet.</i><br><br>Only Soel can hear them. But for now, all anyone can do is listen, and remember that this ship carries more than its own crew.",
      8: "The sky changes before the wind does. This is not a normal storm. The clouds close in fast, the wind patterns make no sense, and Mezstorm closes both eyes and listens, not to the wind, but to what moves beneath it.<br><br>It is not angry. It is calling. Not with words, but with intent.<br><br>The crew goes quiet. They trust that instinct. Sometimes the answers we seek do not come in calm seas. They come in storms. And sometimes we have to be the one who listens.",
      9: "The storm’s call grows stronger, and Mezstorm feels it inside, a pull that splits and unravels. For a moment control slips. The storm form breaks through, faster and sharper, but colder and more distant.<br><br>Then Mezstorm stops fighting, and listens. Same thoughts, same memories, same fears, same hopes. Two forms, one self. <i>I understand now. You are not my enemy.</i><br><br>When the storm eases, nothing is lost. Both forms move together, not in control but in balance. Some storms break people. But some help them become whole.",
      10: "Senedra’s warning was right. They were not alone.<br><br>A shape older than the maps breaks the surface, and the sea monster rises from the dark water. It is not just a creature. It is a guardian.<br><br>Joel holds the front and shields the crew. Aisyah moves with precision, Zaki and the deck crew fight with everything they have, and Eliz keeps them standing as the tentacles come again and again. At last it dives back into the depths, wounded but not defeated.<br><br>“It was only the beginning,” San says. It leaves behind a message, and something waiting below.",
      11: "With the monster behind them, the waters finally go still. They descend into a city lost to the sea, older than any kingdom they know.<br><br>At the heart of the ruins they find what the monster guarded: not gold, not weapons, but an ancient navigation device. Eliz says she has never seen anything so advanced. When Mezstorm touches it, they feel a memory that is not theirs.<br><br>Aisyah is cautious. Zaki would rather walk away than risk the crew. They leave it where it lies. Some treasures are not meant to be taken, and some answers wait for the right time.",
      12: "Beneath the ledger and the seal, Aisyah finds a rolled map wrapped in oilcloth, older than the ship. It shows familiar seas, but beyond them only stars, symbols and a place with no name.<br><br>Joel studies the markings, looking for a practical explanation. Zaki is cautious: some secrets are safer left buried. Mezstorm senses something in the symbols, a pull toward whatever was calling during the storm. Soel sits calmly on the edge of the map, ears twitching, and signals no danger, only recognition.<br><br>Aisyah suggests they keep it. For now, the map stays with the crew.",
      13: "The crew gathers around the map. It leads to no known port. It points to coordinates beyond the charted seas, and no one knows who left it, or whether it is a trap.<br><br>Mezstorm feels something in the distance, a pull. Soel sits on the map as though he has already chosen. Zaki trusts the captain’s judgment, and Eliz reminds everyone that courage also means being prepared.<br><br>San makes her decision. Not because the journey is safe, but because some answers are worth finding, and because she trusts her crew. Joel does not say much. His silence is a promise.<br><br>“We sail at first light.”",
      14: "Level fifty, and San looks out over the new horizon. So much has changed since the first day, but the reason remains the same.<br><br>Aisyah laughs at how far a crew that almost did not make it out of the first forest has come. Eliz turns milestones into plans, and Zaki grins at the road ahead. Joel says little, but his presence says more. Soel rests nearby, quiet and steady.<br><br>They mark the map for the next leg: the Sunsprite Coast, with new lands and new people, and new risks. Different paths, same horizon. For today, they allow themselves this moment."
    },
    3: {
      1: "After a long day in port, the crew gathers at the tavern. They drink to new shores, to fewer storms, to maps and the mysteries they hide. Soel agrees, from his place beside the lantern.<br><br>As the night wears on, one by one, they head off to rest, until only San and Joel are left at the table. It is quieter now, and there is an unfamiliar feeling in it that feels strangely familiar.<br><br>“Tonight, just us. No titles. No duties.”<br><br>Outside, the sea is quiet, and for the first time in a long while, San lets herself simply be here.",
      2: "The next morning, Joel catches San on deck. He is not good with long speeches, but he says what matters. He does not want to pretend last night did not happen, and he does not think it was a mistake. He wants this to continue.<br><br>San says it clearly, with no games and no confusion. She is still the Captain. But in this, she wants Joel to take the lead.<br><br>Awkward, honest, and a little funny, but real. Some things never change: she is still the Captain, he is still her First Mate, and now he is also her partner.",
      3: "A few days pass, and life goes on. Meals are cooked and plans are made, but the feeling lingers. San cannot explain it. It is more than comfort. Every time they are close, it feels like something they have done before.<br><br>Joel has the same feeling: a sense of familiarity without a timeline. She wonders if their missing memories are trying to return. Sometimes, in the quiet moments, she sees fragments. A feeling. A touch. A place she cannot name.<br><br>Not a full memory, just enough to know this is not the first time. San chooses the present, not the memories she does not have.",
      4: "Late morning on deck, and San is sparring with Joel. He is holding back, she says, and if he keeps going easy on her, she will not improve. He tells her he is not holding back. He is making sure she does not end up in the med bay before lunch.<br><br>“Then try harder.”<br><br>Steel or wood, it does not matter. San knows when he is serious. She is still catching her breath, but she is smiling, and he is looking at her like he already knew that.<br><br>Not every encounter is with an enemy. Some are with the people who walk beside you.",
      5: "A routine supply run turns into trouble when armed raiders ambush the docks. San takes the left side. Joel takes the front. Different roles, same rhythm, as if they had done this before.<br><br>San’s spells keep the raiders at bay and give the crew room to move. Joel holds the front line and shields the others. In the end, the raiders retreat, leaving behind broken crates and a clear message.<br><br>It is not about finishing them. It is about keeping everyone safe.<br><br>Afterwards, the adrenaline fades. What remains is familiarity. Another day survived, and tomorrow, they sail again.",
      6: "Morning on the Crimson Tide, and plans turn into action. Supplies, repairs and a short run to the next port. Joel is First Mate: he plans, checks, and keeps everyone honest.<br><br>“Aye, Captain.”<br><br>The crew moves with purpose, and experience makes everything smoother. Joel does not just follow orders. He looks ahead, making sure it is done properly. He haggles over rope, nails and oil, and a little kindness goes a long way.<br><br>Good work today, First Mate. Same crew, a different kind of trust.",
      7: "Late afternoon on the Crimson Tide, and after a busy few days at sea the crew finally has a moment to breathe. Different roles, different personalities, the same table.<br><br>“Mez... can I ask you something?” San says. She knows they do not remember a lot about their past. But did they always have this distance between them?<br><br>There were things, Mezstorm admits. They were closer once, then they were not. Complicated things. But San is still here, and that means something.<br><br>Blood makes us sisters. But showing up makes us family.",
      8: "Another day at sea, and the Crimson Tide moves forward, carrying all of them and all the parts they do not remember.<br><br>Sometimes it feels strange, like there are empty spaces in the memory. But when San looks around now, she does not feel empty anymore. Not remembering does not mean it did not happen. It only means they get to write what comes next.<br><br>She used to think their memories made them who they are. But maybe it is the people who stay that really matter. Different worlds, different scars, and we choose each other, again and again.",
      9: "The Crimson Tide docks at a busy port town. Supplies, repairs, and a chance to rest before the next leg of the journey. San checks the equipment and provisions at the supply market, and not everyone who offers to help is a friend.<br><br>A man takes her for a woman travelling alone and offers to buy her dinner. A second will not take no for an answer. Joel does not raise his voice, but it is clear. That is enough.<br><br>San teases him afterwards. He says he was not jealous. It was obvious, she says. He was just being careful.<br><br>New places will always bring new faces. Some are friendly, some are not.",
      10: "Some things are easier to notice than to explain. San was quiet today, and Joel noticed. A woman at the port had seemed interested in him, and San had teased him about it, a little teasing going a long way on a long journey.<br><br>“You know, you can tell me if something bothers you.”<br><br>“Nothing bothers me.”<br><br>“Jealousy is a girl thing.”<br><br>Back on the Crimson Tide, they both admit they are terrible at talking about it. Apparently. Good. Because they already understand each other. Some people become closer by saying everything, and some by learning what the other never says.",
      11: "Morning aboard the Crimson Tide, and the course is set, the wind is good and the supplies are in order. Later, in a quieter moment on deck, San remembers what Joel said back at the tavern. <i>If you ever want to take the lead, you can. I’ll follow your pace.</i><br><br>“You said I could.”<br><br>“Could what?”<br><br>“That.”<br><br>It is not a dramatic moment, just a real one. Then duty calls. “Captain! First Mate! We’re ready to depart!” Back to the crew, back to the mission.<br><br>Sometimes becoming something more does not change what you already are. It just makes the journey a little warmer.",
      12: "A new day, and San says they make a good team. Joel agrees. So this is them, then? Yeah. It took him long enough to say it, and he says he was making sure.<br><br>It is not a grand declaration, just the truth, at last. Still the Captain, still the First Mate, still them. Different titles, the same partnership.<br><br>The crew notices. Aisyah gives them a knowing look, and a quiet voice from across the table says it was about time. Later on deck, it does not feel different. It feels warmer.<br><br>More ports to see, more stories, the same crew, whatever comes next, together. We are not just where we started, and we are not just what we were. We are us.",
      13: "Morning routine on the Crimson Tide. The wind looks good for most of the day, and Joel will adjust the sails after breakfast. Supplies are sorted, the maps are updated, the weapons are clean. Everyone is pulled together, as always.<br><br>By midday the sea changes, and what was calm becomes rough. “Secure everything! Sails to reef! All hands, stations!” San takes the helm, Joel holds steady, and the crew works as one.<br><br>Afterwards, it was not as bad as it could have been. That evening, even with the storm, today was a good day. Same crew, that is what matters. We cannot control the seas, we cannot avoid the storms, but we can choose how we face them.",
      14: "Morning preparations, and Joel notices San has not eaten. Aisyah notices more: the two of them are getting predictable. Some things do not need to be said.<br><br>Care looks different for everyone. A quiet word from Wren to Aldric, who already knows. A sibling jab between Aisyah and Mezstorm, over who put the maps where. Different personalities, the same family.<br><br>That night on deck, San wonders if there is something they should say. Joel says nothing for a while, then tells her that if there is, they will say it. Some things take time, and that is okay. Not everything important needs to be spoken aloud.",
      15: "Everyone carries something. Some of it was chosen, some of it was forced on them, but it is still theirs to carry.<br><br>San asks Joel whether he ever feels like he carries too much. Sometimes, he says, but it is lighter with her here. Aisyah carries regrets, and her family. Mezstorm carries a past that does not always fit who they are now, and also the people who never stopped seeing them. Eliz carries worries, and hope. Senedra and Zaki carry too much, because someone has to.<br><br>We all carry something. We are not alone in what we carry. And no matter the weight, we carry it together.",
      16: "A familiar place. San and Joel look out over the water and say what they both remember: they have been here before. The first time was fear and chaos, and everything felt out of control. They were different then, with less experience and less trust, more alone.<br><br>Now it is the same place with different people, and they are not the same people anymore. For San, it taught her that fear does not have the final word. For Joel, it was a reminder to stay, even when it is hard. Some of the crew lost more than they can replace, and some still carry guilt.<br><br>This time they face it together. We remember the fight, not to stay in the past, but to honour how far we have come.",
      17: "A new day, with the same horizon and different challenges. Next stop is the Stormreach Channel, and San and Joel plan the approach together. The southern route is faster but riskier. The northern route takes longer, but the crew is still recovering and there is no need to push their luck.<br><br>“I make the final call. That is my responsibility. It is not always the easy choice.” San chooses the path for the crew, for tomorrow. Joel says that is why he is there: to keep her steady, to see what she might not see, and to keep the crew together when things get difficult.<br><br>A captain to choose the path, a first mate to stand beside her.",
      18: "A forgotten place, quiet among the ruins. San finds a small locket among the stones, someone’s keepsake, a family’s maybe. The crew wonders what happened to them. Perhaps they moved on. Or perhaps they did not have a choice.<br><br>Not all memories are found in visions or books. Some are left behind in places we once stood. San says it is not just what we achieve, but who we become for the people beside us.<br><br>They leave the locket behind with a simple message. <i>To those who were here: may your story continue in the lives you touched.</i> We carry their story with us now. To keep going, to keep choosing, to keep leaving something good behind.",
      19: "A familiar shore, a hidden path, a quick look. Even the familiar notices what we cannot see. Soel senses something below, and the crew follows a staircase down into the dark, deeper than anyone expected.<br><br>On the wall, an old story: symbols that look like theirs, but older. Beyond it, a hidden chamber glows with a city of light beneath a place they thought they already knew. “Wow. I did not expect this beneath a place we have already been. This changes things.”<br><br>It is beautiful, and a little frightening. Knowledge like this always comes with risks. They document it, and they decide what to do next, together.",
      20: "San tells Joel what she used to think: that she had to be strong on her own, that her job was to protect everyone. If she could handle it by herself, it would be easier for everyone. But that is not how this works anymore.<br><br>Her strength is not in doing everything alone. It is in having him beside her. Different roles, different people, the same direction: Aisyah sees what others miss, Mezstorm stands between past and future, Eliz carries hope, Wren heals what breaks, Aldric stands when it is hard, Senedra keeps moving, Zaki holds the line, and Soel reminds everyone to breathe.<br><br>A captain and a first mate. Neither without the other.",
      21: "A look back. They have come a long way, haven’t they? There were easier choices they could have made, safer ones. But they did not take them. They chose this.<br><br>Every choice has a cost: they lost things along the way, some people, some chances, some versions of themselves. But even the pain shaped who they are now. San asks Joel whether he ever regrets it. No. There are hard days, but he does not regret choosing her. Choosing this crew.<br><br>We chose each other. We chose a different kind of tomorrow. And we would make that choice again. Not by chance, but by choice."
    },
    4: {
      1: "The coastline comes into view, a sprawling harbour, weathered but full of potential. “Land ahead!” “That’s our port.”<br><br>The harbourmaster welcomes them. The docks are worn and many of the buildings are in disrepair, but the port has a strong heart, and it will grow again if people are willing to put their hands to it. If the Crimson Tide is looking for a place to dock, trade, and perhaps help rebuild, they may find friends here.<br><br>San is given docking rights and a base of operations. <i>It is not just a port. It could be a home, for us and for others.</i>",
      2: "A few days after arriving, the crew begins to explore the port. Repairs are underway, supplies are stocked, and life slowly finds a rhythm. And yet, so many new faces, and some of them feel strangely familiar.<br><br>Like old friends seen in another life. A woman says the pattern on San’s coat suits her. An old shipwright tells her the ship is in fine shape, and that his voice feels familiar. The memories are still out of reach, but the connections feel real.<br><br>Back at the tavern, San and Joel compare the day’s fragments. Maybe the answers will come in time. For now they keep sailing, keep meeting people, and stay open to what is ahead.",
      3: "While exploring the market district, San and the crew notice a commotion. A young woman is cornered by thugs trying to steal a map case. Before the situation escalates, a blur of motion and a flash of silver fur changes everything.<br><br>Her name is Iris, a scout, and the white fox at her side is Ash. “That name... it feels familiar,” San says. “I do not know why.” Iris feels it too, like she has heard it before in another life.<br><br>Over a shared meal at the tavern, Iris tells them she travels the roads taking contracts and gathering information. By the end of the night, she agrees to travel with the Crimson Tide, at least for a while.",
      4: "The crew follows a rumour about an old lookout on the hill, a good place to survey the harbour and nearby waters. At the top, they meet a quiet young man who has been observing the port for his own reasons.<br><br>He is cautious at first, but curious. His name is KW Liang, and the white rabbit on his shoulder is Snowball. “Patterns are easier to see from up here.” They compare maps, trade notes and recent sightings, and he shares what he knows about nearby islands, shifting currents and unusual activity in the region.<br><br>As the sun sets, San invites him to join the crew for the next leg of the journey. He hesitates, then smiles. “I’d like that.”",
      5: "As the crew explores the port, they begin to notice something odd: certain people trigger a strange sense of familiarity, even though no clear memories return. Joel looks at KW Liang and feels he has seen him before. Iris says it is like a half-remembered conversation, almost like a dream she did not finish.<br><br>It is not a memory return. No visions, no clear scenes. Just small, familiar feelings, a face, a voice, a mannerism, that tug at something deeper. Eliz wonders whether the Codex is hinting at connections between worlds. Soel agrees that it might be important later.<br><br>The crew decides to observe, stay open, and keep an eye on them.",
      6: "At the harbour, a well-dressed man watches a new ship from the shadows. Robin C, a senior partner at R&C, notices the Crimson Tide as it docks, and something about the crew catches his attention immediately.<br><br>He picks out the two magic users, with clear ability and a presence that makes the ship unusual and desirable. He notes Joel in action at the dock, coordinating the crew with calm efficiency: an experienced First Mate who keeps everyone in line without raising his voice.<br><br>A valuable ship is worth taking, but only when the timing is right. For now, Robin prefers to gather more information: their routes, alliances and habits. A new game has begun.",
      7: "As the Crimson Tide prepares to depart, R&C’s men move in. What seemed a routine evening turns into an ambush. Robin has made his move.<br><br>“Surrender the ship, come work under R&C, and wipe out what you owe. Resistance will only increase the debt.” San answers that this is their home, and they owe nothing. When words fail, Robin shows what he is capable of. His binding magic freezes the deck into a trap of chains and frost, locking down San and Mezstorm, the two he wants most.<br><br>Outmanoeuvred and restrained, the crew is forced to withdraw. “Run if you must. It just increases the interest.” It is not just a fight. It is a contract.",
      8: "Robin makes his move, using binding runes to trap the Crimson Tide’s crew on the deck. The fight he wanted. The data he needed.<br><br>Chains of ice lock the crew in place, and it becomes a brutal test. But San and Mezstorm break free. Together they counter Robin’s bindings, light against storm, and shatter the contract’s hold. “You can bind a moment, but not a tide.” The crew regains its freedom and turns the fight around.<br><br>Overpowered, Robin is forced to retreat. Injured but alive, he refuses to surrender, and escapes under cover of smoke and darkness, leaving behind a warning: this was a setback, not the end. A man who deals in contracts always finds another way.",
      9: "After the battle with Robin, the crew makes port at a coastal town to rest and repair. At the local clinic, they meet a renowned physician with a reputation that travels across many shores. “San? Do you know me?”<br><br>Dr AA recognises San, but parts of the past are missing, like pieces lost to the Crimson Tide world. His memory is not complete, but he never forgot the people who left an impression. Some things may fade, but some people still matter.<br><br>He checks everyone’s injuries and gives advice. “Take care, San. Come back when you can.” Different shores, the same fight, and there are still people who choose to help.",
      10: "As the crew prepares to leave the port, a familiar voice calls out. Mimi, a diviner with a reputation for uncanny insights, joins the crew, bringing news and questions of her own.<br><br>That evening she reads the currents with cards, a star chart and flowing sand. The visions reveal fragments: people, places and a recurring shadow. “The tide remembers. Not just places, but people.” She sees a man, Robin, who leaves a mark wherever he goes. His story is not over, but he is not the only one in the shadows.<br><br>You have all felt it, haven’t you? A familiarity you cannot quite place. “The Crimson Tide world hides and preserves memories differently.” Some are buried, some are rewritten, but they are still there.",
      11: "As the crew docks at a new port, a warm and familiar voice calls out. Mimi, a diviner with a bright smile and a mind that sees beyond the obvious, joins the crew, and this time she is not alone.<br><br>Brada Shah is Mimi’s partner on the road and in life. Their bond is built on friendship, trust, laughter, and the same belief in people, in good stories. That evening, the crew shares a meal, and it does not take long for Mimi and Brada to feel at home. Brada’s music brings more than entertainment: it lifts spirits and eases heavy thoughts.<br><br>Some people arrive as strangers. They leave as family.",
      12: "At the docks, while the crew handles supplies, Jeff approaches Joel with a familiar smile. “Joel. Long time. Didn’t expect to see you here.” Some faces bring back memories, whether we want them or not.<br><br>Jeff gets straight to the point. “Come with me, Joel. Better pay. A bigger ship. A real future. You’ll have a place beside me, not a worker, but a trusted man.” Joel listens, but does not give an answer. From a short distance, San notices the conversation. She does not interrupt, but she watches closely.<br><br>Jeff leaves with a final smile. “Think about it, Joel. My offer still stands.” Not every invitation leads to a better future.",
      13: "Later that night, Jeff finds Joel on deck, returning to the conversation. “Still thinking about my offer, Joel? Join me and you’ll have more than just a bigger ship.” He leans in, laying out more of what he has to offer.<br><br>Joel has given it thought, and his answer is no. When Joel still refuses, Jeff’s tone turns personal. He asks how the captain tastes in bed.<br><br>“That’s enough. Don’t talk about San like that.” It is not a joke. It is disrespectful, and Joel is not interested. He turns and walks away, leaving Jeff behind with his resolve unchanged.<br><br>“Suit yourself, Joel. You’ll regret this.” Some people show you who they really are when you say no. And some doors close so the right ones can open.",
      14: "While the crew rests at the coastal camp, Jeff and his men land under cover of night. Torches cut through the dark. “Surprise, little crew. Time to collect what’s mine.”<br><br>Jeff’s men surge forward, overpowering the outer guards. Jeff enters the fray himself, fighting with brutal strength, using his size and weight to crush and overpower. Joel meets him head-on, defending and countering, focusing on precision rather than brute force. “You picked the wrong crew.”<br><br>San moves to protect the crew with her magic, creating openings and keeping everyone safe. After a fierce exchange, Jeff realises he will not break the crew tonight, and with heavy losses he calls a retreat. “This isn’t over. Remember this night.”",
      15: "After the recent battles, another captain approaches Joel with a better offer: higher pay, a larger crew, more opportunities. A logical offer, a practical offer. “I’ll think about it.”<br><br>That night, Joel sits with San, away from the noise. “You don’t have to answer now.” “But I need to know what you’re thinking.” It is not a test. It is a conversation between people who trust each other.<br><br>Joel reflects on what he has found with the Crimson Tide, something more valuable than pay: a crew that fights together, people who believe in each other, a family that does not leave anyone behind. The next morning he gives his answer. “I appreciate the offer. But I’m staying with Crimson Tide. This is where I’m meant to be.”",
      16: "In the days after the raid, more allies arrive, some by invitation, some by coincidence, and some because they chose to. “Good to see you all again.” It feels like a real crew now.<br><br>From scouts and healers to traders and craftsmen, the circle continues to grow. As night falls, the crew gathers to share food, stories and plans. It is no longer just work. It is family.<br><br>San steps out onto the dock later that night, looking at the crew as they work and rest around her. “This isn’t just a group of recruits anymore. It feels like an extended crew. A family. Different people, different paths, but we’re all heading toward the same horizon.”",
      17: "At the dock, the crew meets new allies and old acquaintances from different places. Some faces feel strangely familiar. “It’s nice to meet you. I don’t know why... but you feel familiar.” “You do? That’s interesting.”<br><br>Later, the same phenomenon happens again, and again. It is like recognising a song without remembering the words. It is not just one person. It keeps happening, across different ports, traders and travellers.<br><br>That night, San looks through the Codex, hoping for clarity. They feel familiar, but the memories do not return. In the end, the crew chooses to accept what they feel, even without the answers. We may not remember everything, but we recognise the important thing.",
      18: "The crew returns to Fair Tide, a port that now feels less like a stopover, and more like a second home. “Back again, Fair Tide. Feels good.” They stock up on rations, potions and spare parts, and get the ship patched, refitted and ready for the next journey.<br><br>Fair Tide offers more than supplies: information, rare goods and new quests from traders, guilds and locals. A new route has just opened, with unusual activity in the western isles. Old contacts, friendly faces and new allies strengthen the Crimson Tide’s growing network. “If you ever need assistance, dock space, intel or crew recommendations, Fair Tide is here for you.”<br><br>It is not just work. Fair Tide is where the crew eats, rests, shares stories, and simply enjoys being together.",
      19: "News travels fast. At Fair Tide, the crew hears that both Robin and Jeff are already moving again. “Robin’s firm is restructuring.” “Jeff’s already back at SK*.” Neither of them is staying down for long.<br><br>Robin regroups, leaning on his network and reputation. People have short memories, he says. As long as he delivers results, they will come back. This time he plays the long game. Jeff rebuilds too, more cautious but with the same patterns. Each time he rebuilds, this place’s magic makes him shift a bit. Sometimes less, sometimes more.<br><br>The crew stays alert. “We’ve come too far to be caught off guard.” Rivals may return, but the tide still moves forward.",
      20: "At Fair Tide, the allies gather to talk openly about what travelling with the Crimson Tide means. “We all came here different ways. But we’re here now, at the same table. So let’s be honest. What do you each want from here?”<br><br>Aisyah confirms her place: she is staying, for her family, and for the next journey. Joel makes his choice clear, quiet but certain. “I’m with you. Same as always.” Eliz and Senedra decide to continue, each for their own reasons. Zaki says he is staying, with his strength and experience. Mezstorm, after their own journey, reaffirms their place: there are answers out there, and they want to find them together.<br><br>A crew worth following is not built by chance. It is built by choice.",
      21: "At Fair Tide, the crew gathers one last time before setting sail. More faces, more stories, the same purpose. Look at all of you. We have come a long way. Not just a crew. A family.<br><br>They remember the battles, the losses, and the lessons that brought them here: from the Leviathan in the deep, to the Planarch in the spire, to Robin and Jeff. Each battle shaped them, and each challenge brought them closer. Every ally adds something unique, and together they are more than the sum of their parts.<br><br>Supplies are loaded, repairs complete, and the course is set. “Ready?” “Always.” “Let’s see what’s next.” New seas. New lands. New stories."
    },
    5: {
      1: "While sailing near one of the ports, San spots an unusual cove tucked between the cliffs. “Look at that. Shall we take a closer look?” “Yes.”<br><br>Inside the cove, she finds an ancient rune embedded in the rock. San steps onto it, and waits, and nothing happens. Maybe it only works with something else? Then Joel arrives and joins her, and when both stand on it, the rune activates. A strange cracking sound echoes through the cavern, and they both feel slightly refreshed.<br><br>“Did you feel that?” “A little.” Neither of them fully understands what happened. They leave the cove carrying the feeling with them, but no answers yet.",
      2: "After several voyages, San and Joel begin to notice something subtle. We have been on a lot of runs together lately. Have you noticed anything different?<br><br>When they are together, they seem to recover more quickly than usual after battles. Their coordination in battle feels unusually good, almost effortless. They move together more naturally, with fewer words and fewer mistakes, like they already know what the other will do.<br><br>San thinks they are just getting better at fighting together. Joel is not convinced. It feels like more than that, he says, but he does not know what it is. Maybe it is nothing. Some things cannot be explained yet. But they are real.",
      3: "While travelling along a narrow coastal pass, the crew is attacked by a pack of shadow-tide marauders. “They’re more than we expected!” “Stay close to me!”<br><br>Joel triggers Shield Wall, standing firm and taking the brunt of the assault. Blades strike, arrows fall, the wall holds. San remains behind him, casting without interruption. And as Joel maintains Shield Wall, San feels a surge of power flow through her, stronger, clearer, more stable. “My magic feels stronger.” “Is it because of my Shield Wall?” “I think so.”<br><br>With Joel’s defence, San unleashes a powerful area spell, turning the tide of the battle. Not just survival. A new kind of strength.<br><br><b>Bonded by the Tide</b> is now unlocked.",
      4: "During a prolonged battle, the crew is surrounded by a relentless wave of shadow-tide creatures. “They just keep coming!” “Stay close to me!”<br><br>Joel pushes Shield Wall beyond its normal limits, standing firm and taking the full force of the assault to protect San. Again. And again. And again. San feels a surge of power flow through her. “It’s different. My magic feels stronger. Like I can reach further.”<br><br>When the last of the enemies fall, the crew catches its breath. Later, as Joel removes his shirt, they notice a change: a mark on his skin that was not there before. It only glows faintly when the bond is active. “So it leaves a mark.” Sometimes bonds leave a mark. Not all scars hurt.",
      5: "During a voyage, the crew faces a powerful enemy unlike any they have fought. A massive attack is aimed at San. “San! Look out!”<br><br>Without hesitation, Joel channels the mark. The tattoo flares to life, and a shield of light blooms around them both. “Joel?!” “It’s not just defence. It’s the bond, making space for her to keep going.” The attack hits, but the shield holds. San is protected. “It worked.” “You protected me.”<br><br>After the battle, Joel studies the mark and realises it can be activated once per day. “First Mate’s Aegis. It feels right. I’m not becoming someone else. I’m just doing what I already do.” San smiles, understanding. A stronger bond, a deeper trust, and many more horizons to explore.",
      6: "The sea is unnaturally calm. Not a ripple. Not a breeze. Then, without warning, the Crimson Tide’s sails fill with a powerful wind, and as suddenly as it came, the wind disappears. “It’s completely still again. What in the tides was that?”<br><br>Not far away, they spot a small vessel and a single figure surrounded by swirling arcane currents. “Ah! An audience! Perfect timing!” His name is Renn, a humble mage conducting important research on wind currents and arcane flow, and he is clearly enjoying himself far too much. Guilty, he admits, when asked. The sea is a marvellous place for experiments.<br><br>San is wary but intrigued. “He’s clearly skilled. And dangerous if bored.” Joel thinks he might be useful. They will keep an eye on him.",
      7: "On a quiet stretch of sea, Renn decides to show what arcane mischief can do. “Right then! Let’s try something fun. Sailing doesn’t have to be boring, does it?” He conjures distant lights on the horizon, phantom ships that are not really there. He summons a burst of wind, filling the sails, then lets it vanish just as quickly. In moments, the Crimson Tide’s appearance shifts under his magic, and ropes move and tie themselves.<br><br>With a few gestures he creates illusory cargo, crates and barrels, anything they need. What you see isn’t always what you get. He leaves confusing trails, multiple illusions moving in different directions. Mischief, not malice. A trickster’s art.",
      8: "Renn joins the Crimson Tide, at least for now. “Thanks for having me! This ship is amazing!” He wastes no time exploring every corner of the ship, and it does not take long before something goes wrong. Whoops! He was just testing a levitation rune. That is the third time today.<br><br>San has concerns. Renn is clever, but unpredictable, and she starts to wonder if bringing him aboard was a mistake. “You invited him,” Joel reminds her. Later that day, someone calls out in alarm: wait, where did the ship go? Renn has managed to make the Crimson Tide temporarily invisible.<br><br>“Okay, okay. Maybe next time I’ll warn everyone first.” For now, Renn stays. The crew can’t deny his talent.",
      9: "A research guild in the next port would love to have Renn. He could leave at any time. “I’m staying. I like it here.” It isn’t destiny, and it isn’t obligation. It is a genuine choice.<br><br>He likes the crew. They are kind, capable, and endlessly interesting. The Crimson Tide is always moving, and that means there is always something new to see, learn, and explore. San gives him space to be curious, without trying to control every strange magical phenomenon. “As long as it doesn’t sink the ship or get us killed, I’m happy for you to explore.”<br><br>Somewhere along the way, the Crimson Tide stopped being just a ship and started feeling like home. “So yeah. I’m not leaving anytime soon.”",
      10: "A young woman arrives at the port, asking for San and Joel. Her name is Jovie. She says she heard about their charitable work and wanted to meet them, but there is a familiarity in her eyes, even though she cannot explain it. “It’s just... I feel like I know you both. Like we’ve met before.”<br><br>Jovie remembers fragments, but no coherent history: a supermarket, medical supplies, a medical centre. The memories do not connect. San recognises her. “Jovie...? It’s you. I remember you.” Joel reacts more strongly, even though he has no memory of what they were.<br><br>Jovie is overwhelmed. “I’m sorry, I should remember, but I can’t.” San and Joel assure her she is safe. “Take your time.” For now, what matters is that she is here.",
      11: "Jovie’s arrival stirs something in both San and Joel. “You... you’re Jovie.” “It’s been a while. I’m so happy to see you both.”<br><br>Scenes start to surface for Joel, not everything, just fragments. He worked at the warehouse, and often delivered supplies to the supermarket where Jovie worked. He does not remember a long conversation, but he remembers her smile. San knew her too: her office was near the supermarket, and she saw Jovie often. They worked in different places, but the same familiar face.<br><br>“Funny how we kept meeting in the most unexpected places.” It is enough to know she mattered. People make a place, and that is what home really is.",
      12: "Jovie opens her medical box, showing the tools and supplies she has kept with her. Bandages, basic medicine, herbal preparations. Practical tools. “It’s not much, but it’s what I have.”<br><br>She explains how she has been surviving using what she can remember: basic treatment, herbs and common remedies. It is not everything, but it is enough to help people. Dr AA immediately recognises her skills and her value to the crew. “You have a good understanding of practical medicine. This is very useful.”<br><br>Jovie also helps with general care, checking on the crew, treating injuries, and advising on health. She may not be a fighter, but her presence makes the crew stronger. Some memories are still missing, but she has found a purpose.",
      13: "Jovie sits on the deck, looking out at the sea. She thinks about the fragments she has, and what she still does not remember. “I may not remember everything... but I remember enough.”<br><br>What she remembers most clearly is the people: San, Joel, and how they made her feel. Working with you. Feeling safe. And that you cared. She realises she does not need to recover her entire old life. She can build a new one, here. “I used to think I needed all my memories back to move forward. But maybe that’s not true. I can choose this. I can choose to stay.”<br><br>San tells her she does not have to remember everything to be part of the crew. What matters is that she is here with them. Dr AA is glad to have her. Jovie chooses the present.",
      14: "San looks out over Fair Tide, seeing it with new eyes. What was once just a port between voyages now feels different. It is not just a stop anymore. People rely on this place now.<br><br>The crew goes about their work, and San notices the growing community they have made. Locals speak about how the Crimson Tide has made a difference: since they started coming here, there is more trade, more work, the port feels alive again. People count on you now.<br><br>That evening, the crew discusses what Fair Tide could become. What if we built something permanent here? A proper base of operations? It means more responsibility, more people depending on us, more risks. But maybe it is worth it. “A port worth keeping.”",
      15: "Gino arrives at Fair Tide, looking for work. His easy smile and warm personality make an immediate impression, and he quickly offers to help in the galley. “Need an extra pair of hands? I can cook, clean, chop, or carry, whatever helps.”<br><br>Gino joins Fair Tide as an assistant cook. He brings energy, laughter, and surprisingly good food. He and Joel end up working together in the galley, and their conversations feel unexpectedly familiar. “You cut vegetables the same way...” “Yeah?” “Feels familiar. Like we’ve worked together before.” A small work-related memory fragment surfaces for both of them.<br><br>“Don’t worry. I don’t need to remember everything, I just know you were someone I worked with. That’s enough.” Some connections do not need the past to be meaningful.",
      16: "Wahyu arrives at Fair Tide with rolls of cloth, travel goods, and a proposal: a small trading stall to supply the crew and the locals. “Good day! I’m Wahyu. I have clothes, travel gear and everyday supplies.” “That sounds useful. Welcome to Fair Tide.”<br><br>When Wahyu and Joel speak, a brief memory fragment surfaces. “Wait... I know you, right? We worked together before?” Not every memory returns, but some faces are still familiar. He sets up his stall with robes, jackets, work clothes and bags, practical and durable.<br><br>Soon after, Dudin arrives, bringing his own skills and energy. He is practical, reliable, and always seen in his army-style gear. “Name’s Dudin. Happy to help out where I can.” Not everyone fights on the front lines. Some keep everything running.",
      17: "A man in camo sets up shop at Fair Tide, unloading crates filled with practical supplies: uniforms, boots, camping gear, tools and more. “Good gear keeps good people going. That’s what I believe. Name’s Dudin. Army supplies, camping gear, tools, the works.”<br><br>When Dudin and Joel talk, a memory fragment surfaces. It is not everything, but it is enough to recognise each other. “You... Joel, right? We worked together before, didn’t we?” “Yeah... I think so. It’s been a long time.” He even remembers Jeff’s name coming up: same idiot, different world, perhaps.<br><br>With Dudin’s business, Fair Tide has another essential piece. Supplies today, a stronger community tomorrow. The port grows stronger, not just through battles, but through the people who choose to build here.",
      18: "As the crew goes about their work at Fair Tide, a familiar face appears at the docks, hesitating for a moment, then smiling. “San? It’s really you?” “Imah? Of course it’s me! Come here!” “You look amazing!”<br><br>Imah remembers the early days, when San taught her the job from the ground up. “You taught me everything, you know? From the basic filing, to dealing with clients, to not panicking when things got messy!” She has been travelling, doing odd administrative work, and helping at different ports. When she heard about Fair Tide, she decided to find San. “I’d like to stay and help here. I can handle administration, records, supplies, whatever you need.”<br><br>A warm reunion, not a fraught one.",
      19: "Nurul arrives at Fair Tide, nervous but hopeful. She heard about the crew from Imah, and decided to come. “Um... San? It’s really you, right? I wasn’t sure.”<br><br>She remembers being San’s clerk. She was slow, and it took a long time to get used to everything. “You were new. And you worked hard. That’s what I remember.” Nurul also remembers being transferred later, and having to deal with Robin’s sarcasm. It is uncomfortable, and incomplete. “I just felt like I could never do anything right.”<br><br>San does not defend herself, and does not try to rewrite what happened. “I’m sorry you had to go through that.” “You did your best with what you had.” Nurul decides to stay. “I’d like to stay, if you’ll have me.”",
      20: "A new boat docks at Fair Tide, and a familiar figure waves from the bow. “San! Long time no see!” It is Dre, arriving with his partner, carrying more bags than strictly necessary, and the smell of fresh coffee beans. “I brought coffee! Priorities, right?”<br><br>Dre remembers San most clearly through coffee. “You’re the one who kept the office alive with coffee. You always knew when we needed one, even before we did.” He is happy to see Imah and Nurul again, his old batchmates, and the three of them have always kept in touch in small ways. “You two look amazing!”<br><br>He has a legal background and is skilled with documents, contracts and trade regulations: exactly what Fair Tide needs. “You handle the adventure. I’ll handle the paperwork.”",
      21: "A new face arrives at Fair Tide, carrying a tool case and a curious look, already scanning the ship. “This is... actually impressive. How does it all work?” His name is Jorvin, a former colleague, and a former mechanic.<br><br>He wastes no time examining the Crimson Tide: the rigging, the engines, the tools, the systems. “Interesting. This design is more efficient than the old models.” He remembers almost nothing clearly, but when he sees San, something clicks. “You.” “Me?” “Yeah. You.” It is not a full memory, just enough to recognise her. “You always broke interesting things.” “Apparently I still do.”<br><br>Jorvin joins Fair Tide as the ship’s tinkerer and mechanic. “So... this is my new office.” “I may not remember everything. But this... this feels like home.”",
      22: "As the crew settles into Fair Tide, Jorvin starts mentioning people he used to work with. “There were others... people from my department. Different roles, different jobs. I don’t remember everything clearly, but I remember the names.”<br><br>Aisy, quiet, capable, always one step ahead. Zul, who keeps things moving and knows people everywhere. Lewis, always has something useful, a good negotiator. Jonathan, deals in rare goods, talks a lot, and somehow always finds good routes. “Want to hear who they are?”<br><br>It is not just a few people. Jorvin’s connections reach well beyond Fair Tide. The crew realises that this is bigger than they expected. These were not just co-workers. They were people who helped each other, and looked out for one another. Fair Tide is becoming a meeting point.",
      23: "The new arrivals settle into their roles. Good food keeps people going. Clothes for the journey, repairs for what comes next. Finding, sourcing, keeping us stocked. Records, schedules, a smoother tomorrow. Healing people and preparing for what is next. Fixing what breaks and building what is next. Moving people, moving things, connecting places. Buying, selling, bringing opportunities our way.<br><br>Aisyah scouts the roads for news and a safer tomorrow. Renn studies arcane research: knowledge, possibilities, new worlds. Joel keeps Fair Tide moving and a crew that sails together.<br><br>“We’re no longer just maintaining a port...” “We’re building a home base.” People, places, skills, stories. A wider horizon. It is more than a port now. It is a home base.",
      24: "Fair Tide is alive: shops, workshops, people and plans. It is no longer an empty port. While we sail, Fair Tide remains. This is our home base.<br><br>Jovie restocks the bandages and checks the antidotes. Gino discovers his spice stock has been raided again: “This is the third missing crate this week!” Renn defends his magic sails as innovation, and Jorvin, looking over the rigging, says he can make it better. “Technically cheating?” “Call it what you will. It still works.”<br><br>San and Joel stand together. “We came here looking for somewhere to stop.” “And stayed?” “We built something.” The faint glow of Joel’s tattoo is a reminder of their bond. The Crimson Tide sails with a stronger crew. This is what we built, and this is only the beginning."
    }
  };
  window.EARLY_CHAPTER_SCENES = SCENES;

  function chapterTitle(arc, id){
    const list = window['ARC' + arc + '_CHAPTERS'] || [];
    const ch = list.find(function(c){ return c.id === id; });
    return ch ? ch.title : ('Chapter ' + id);
  }
  function showScene(entry, tries){
    const m = document.getElementById('modalOverlay');
    if (m && m.classList.contains('active') && (tries || 0) < 80) { setTimeout(function(){ showScene(entry, (tries || 0) + 1); }, 700); return; }
    if (typeof window.showStoryModal === 'function') window.showStoryModal(entry);
  }
  // Fight chapters complete by winning a fight (Arc IV 8 and 14, Arc V 3 to 5): the win marks the chapter read, so the scene follows it.
  const FIGHT_CHAPTERS = { 4: [8, 14], 5: [3, 4, 5] };
  let pending = false;
  [2, 3, 4, 5].forEach(function(arc){
    const fname = 'markArc' + arc + 'ChapterRead', pname = 'comicProgress' + arc;
    const old = window[fname];
    if (typeof old !== 'function') return;
    window[fname] = function(id){
      const self = this, args = arguments, n = Number(id);
      const isRead = function(){ return !!(game[pname] && game[pname][n]); };
      const scene = SCENES[arc][n];
      const isFight = (FIGHT_CHAPTERS[arc] || []).indexOf(n) !== -1;
      const stateFn = window['arc' + arc + 'ObjectiveState'];
      const current = typeof stateFn === 'function' && stateFn() === ('complete_arc' + arc + '_chapter_' + n);
      if (isRead() || !scene || isFight || !current) {
        // already read, no scene, locked, or a fight win: behave as before (a fight win shows its scene after the XP)
        const before = isRead();
        const r = old.apply(self, args);
        try {
          if (isFight && !before && isRead() && scene) {
            const entry = { title: chapterTitle(arc, n), blurb: scene };
            setTimeout(function(){ showScene(entry, 0); }, 500);
          }
        } catch(e) {}
        return r;
      }
      // a normal chapter: the scene first, and the chapter is marked read (XP and all) when Continue is pressed
      const mo = document.getElementById('modalOverlay');
      if (pending && mo && mo.classList.contains('active')) return;   // already showing
      pending = true;
      const done = function(){
        pending = false;
        if (typeof closeModal === 'function') closeModal();
        old.apply(self, args);
      };
      if (typeof showModal === 'function') showModal(chapterTitle(arc, n), scene, [{ text: 'Continue', action: done }]);
      else done();
    };
  });

  // The Story screen's button for these arcs reads "Read Chapter" rather than "Mark Chapter Read".
  function relabel(){
    document.querySelectorAll('#storyContent button[onclick^="markArc2ChapterRead"], #storyContent button[onclick^="markArc3ChapterRead"], #storyContent button[onclick^="markArc4ChapterRead"], #storyContent button[onclick^="markArc5ChapterRead"]').forEach(function(btn){
      if (btn.textContent.indexOf('Read Chapter') === -1) btn.textContent = '📖 Read Chapter';
    });
  }
  function watch(){
    const box = document.getElementById('storyContent');
    if (!box || box.__earlySceneWatch) return;
    box.__earlySceneWatch = true;
    new MutationObserver(relabel).observe(box, { childList: true, subtree: true });
    relabel();
  }
  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', watch, { once: true }); else watch();
})();
